<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny. I'm Happy!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Illustrate the main ideas from a reading in a creative project. Reading: Summarize a text using a graphic organizer. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can illustrate main ideas from reading about weather by drawing pictures (e.g., a sun for "It's sunny."). Can summarize weather texts with a graphic organizer (e.g., "It's sunny" under "weather category"). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable weather-related words (e.g., blending /ˈr-eɪn-i/ in "rainy" and /s-n-oʊ/ in "snow").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge of weather and feelings vocabulary from Lesson 1.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, class! How do you feel today?" The teacher shows flashcards of different weather conditions (sun, rain, cloud, wind) and asks, "What's the weather like today?" Students respond orally with previously learned vocabulary (e.g., "It's sunny!"). The teacher shows a happy face and says, "I'm happy!" and then a sad face, "I'm sad."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a "sunny" flashcard and says clearly, "It's sunny. I feel happy!" The teacher then writes "It's sunny" and "I'm happy" on the board, pointing to the contraction 's in "It's" and 'm in "I'm." The teacher models blending phonemes for a familiar weather word: /s/ - /u/ - /n/ -> "sun." The teacher points to a picture of rain and says, "When it's rainy, I get wet. I need my umbrella."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sunny' about clouds or the sun?" (Expected: The sun!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I feel happy or sad when it's sunny?" (Expected: Happy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's this word?" (pointing to "rainy" on a flashcard) "Is it /r-eɪn/ or /s-u-n/?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day." Focus on a short reading text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated short story or narrative excerpt projected on the board or provided as a handout. The text will include sentences like: "This is Lily. Today is a beautiful day! It's sunny and bright. Lily feels happy. She can play outside. When it's rainy, Lily feels a little sad. She stays inside and reads. When it's windy, Lily feels cold. She needs her warm coat. When it's cloudy, Lily feels lazy. She likes to watch the sky."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student understands short, simple written texts about familiar concrete topics related to daily life and personal experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the story about? Is it about food or weather?" (Expected: Weather!).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads sentences and students say "True" or "False" based on the text. For example: "It's sunny and Lily feels sad." (False). "Lily plays outside when it's rainy." (False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match short sentences from the text (e.g., "It's sunny.") with corresponding pictures of weather or feelings.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading skills, specifically identifying main ideas and practicing phonemic awareness. Using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences with contractions (I'm, It's), Present Simple (I feel, It's), WH-questions ("How do you feel?"), and vocabulary (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, happy, sad, cold, coat, umbrella).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Reading Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences from the story, but with a key weather or feeling word missing. Students read the sentence and choose the correct word from a provided word bank to complete it.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "It's _____. Lily feels happy." (Word bank: sunny, rainy).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "When it's windy, Lily feels _____." (Word bank: warm, cold).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Reading for Key Words (Circle the Weather):</b> Students receive a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a different day's weather and feelings. They read the text and circle all the weather words (nouns: sun, rain, cloud, wind; adjectives: sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and feeling words (happy, sad, cold, lazy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness (Blending & Reading):</b> The teacher writes phonemes on the board or uses flashcards for words like 'sun', 'rain', 'rainy', 'windy'. The teacher says the phonemes slowly (e.g., /r/-/eɪ/-/n/) and students blend them to form the word "rain" by saying it aloud. Then, students read the written word. This is repeated for 'sunny' (/s/-/ʌ/-/n/-/i/) and 'windy' (/w/-/ɪ/-/n/-/d/-/i/). The teacher explicitly highlights the /s/ sound in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy" while reading.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Pair Reading and Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading short sentences (e.g., "It's sunny. I feel happy." or "It's rainy. I need my umbrella.") to their partner. The partner points to a corresponding picture or drawing of the weather or item. They check each other's reading clarity and word recognition.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day." Focus on demonstrating reading comprehension through illustration and summarization.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Creative project - "My Weather and Feelings Graphic Organizer." Students will create a simple graphic organizer that illustrates main ideas from short weather descriptions. This helps them summarize the text creatively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a template for a graphic organizer. The template has four boxes, each with a different simple sentence describing weather and a feeling or action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentences:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "It's sunny. I'm happy!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "It's rainy. I feel sad."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. "It's windy. I feel cold."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. "It's cloudy. I feel lazy."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each sentence independently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For each sentence, students draw a picture that illustrates the main idea (e.g., a sun and a smiling face for "It's sunny. I'm happy!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After drawing, students can optionally write the weather word from each sentence under their drawing to summarize.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, offering support and asking "What's the weather like here?" and "How do you feel?" to encourage use of target language.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of reading comprehension and ability to illustrate main ideas, with student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist (for reading comprehension and accurate illustration) and quick teacher check of completed graphic organizers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students show their "My Weather and Feelings Graphic Organizer" to the teacher. They point to each drawing and read the corresponding sentence or simply name the weather and feeling (e.g., "Sunny. Happy."). This allows the teacher to assess both reading and comprehension through visual representation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of illustrations in depicting the main idea of each sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation when reading the sentences or naming the weather/feelings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target vocabulary (e.g., sun, happy, rain, sad).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to blend phonemes for familiar weather words when prompted by the teacher.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete one of the following sentences on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can read about the weather and draw a picture for 'It's _____.'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I know how to blend the sounds in the word '_____'."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, and connection to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What weather word was easy to read and draw today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What makes you feel happy on a sunny day?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Did students successfully illustrate the main ideas from the simple reading texts? Were they able to blend phonemes for weather words? What reading strategies (e.g., matching pictures to text, completing sentences) worked best for this grade level? Were the explanations of contractions clear? What adjustments are needed for supporting weaker readers in the next lesson? Scaffolds might include providing partially drawn pictures or focusing on fewer sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson will focus on Speaking & Interaction. We will use the weather and feeling vocabulary learned today to ask and answer questions about how people feel in different weather conditions. Students will practice polite phrases like "How do you feel?" and "I feel happy, thank you."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>